WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.989 --> 00:00:25.939
So yesterday with Michael Bloomberg first
debate as the democratic presidential candidate

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:26.939
here

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.969
finally got the polling support that he needed
in order to get into a debate. Now that of

00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:36.629
course the DNC has eliminated that individual
donor requirement as a prerequisite for getting

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:44.469
into the debates and Michael Bloomberg was
eaten alive. I mean just absolutely annihilated

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:48.939
in what could be a campaign ending debate.
I don't know. It might not be, and we're going

00:00:48.939 --> 00:00:52.290
to talk about that a little bit later. Depends
on how many people watched the debate. It

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:57.389
depends how many people are sort of staking
their support on Michael Bloomberg based on

00:00:57.389 --> 00:01:02.170
something like debate performance rather than
for other reasons. We'll get to that. We were

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:09.170
wondering will it be the centrists attacking
Bloomberg because he is more of a direct threat

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:14.850
to them. Would it be the Progressive's Bernie
and Warren trying to take advantage of having

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.570
a turbo billionaire on the stage to talk about
how radically different their policies are?

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.840
And the answer was yes, yes to all of it.
Yes. To all of the above. Everybody attacked

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.670
Bloomberg centrists in one way, the Progressive's
in another way. And I'm going to show you

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.630
some of those moments now before we do this,
if you had pulled me aside yesterday, five

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:39.439
minutes before the debate and said to me,
David, if you had to guess, do you think Elizabeth

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:46.979
Warren will mention horse faced lesbians in
the first 20 seconds of the debate later in

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:51.689
the debate or not at all? I probably would
have said, I don't expect Elizabeth Warren

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:55.939
to mention Horseface lesbians and I would've
been wrong within the first 20 seconds at

00:01:55.939 --> 00:02:01.369
her first opportunity to talk Elizabeth Warren
going right in on Michael Bloomberg with alleged

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:03.909
comments that he's made about women. Take
a look

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:09.190
for another four years and we can't stand
that. So I'd like to talk about who we're

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:16.930
running against. A billionaire who calls women
fat broads and horse faced lesbians. And no,

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:23.100
I'm not talking about Donald Trump. I'm talking
about mayor Bloomberg. Democrats are not going

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.920
to win. Just

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:32.579
immediately setting the tone for what was
going to be a night of slamming Michael Bloomberg

00:02:32.579 --> 00:02:38.000
without Bloomberg defending himself particularly
well and he was taking hits left and right.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.079
It was nonstop. The worst moment for Bloomberg
was when Elizabeth Warren started pressing

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:50.359
him about nondisclosure agreements with former
employees related to sexual harassment, most

00:02:50.359 --> 00:02:54.580
of which we believe, I mean we don't know
cause they're NDA, but we believe most of

00:02:54.580 --> 00:03:01.489
it was between other, uh, employees. In other
words, it was at Bloomberg's company but didn't

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:06.079
involve Bloomberg. Although eventually Bloomberg
talks about, you know, jokes I told that people

00:03:06.079 --> 00:03:11.070
may not have liked. And, uh, Bloomberg was
not well prepared for this. Uh, really, really

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:14.870
brutal, brutal. And again, Elizabeth Warren
just really destroying him.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:19.009
Senator Warren even critical available above
on this issue. Yes, I have. And I hope you

00:03:19.009 --> 00:03:23.049
heard what his defense was. I've been nice
to some women.

00:03:23.049 --> 00:03:24.430
[inaudible]

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:34.150
the mayor has to stand on his record and what
we need to know is exactly what's lurking

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:41.680
out there. He has gotten some number of women,
dozens who knows to sign nondisclosure agreements,

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.990
both for sexual harassment and for gender
discrimination in the workplace. So mr mayor,

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:53.060
are you willing to release all of those women
from those nondisclosure agreements so we

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.760
can hear their side of the story?

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:04.729
[inaudible] we have a very few

00:04:04.729 --> 00:04:10.340
nondisclosure agreements. How many, how many
is that? None of them accused me of doing

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:16.560
anything other than maybe they didn't like
a joke I told and let me just pause and let

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.560
me put,

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.050
so this is already terrible. I did nothing
other than maybe somebody didn't like a joke

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:26.430
I told. That's terrible. That's a, I couldn't
think of a worse way to try to explain what

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.400
happened. Typically jokes don't lead to payoffs
and nondisclosure agreements. It's already

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.610
bad for Michael Bloomberg. But let's continue.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:39.050
Maybe they didn't like the joke I told and
let me just pause and let me put, these would

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:44.301
be agreements between two parties that wanted
to keep it quiet and that's up to them. They

00:04:44.301 --> 00:04:48.490
sign those agreements and we'll live with
it. So wait, when you say it, it's up to you.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:56.379
I just want to be clear. Some is how many
and when you, and when you say they signed

00:04:56.379 --> 00:05:02.849
them and they wanted them, if they wish now
to speak out and tell their side of the story

00:05:02.849 --> 00:05:07.569
about what it is they alleged, that's now
okay with you. You're releasing them on television

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:09.810
tonight, Senator

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:10.930
[inaudible],

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:18.770
son of a company, and somebody else in this
case, the man or a woman or could be more

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.860
than that. They decided when they made an
agreement that they wanted to keep it quiet

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.319
for everybody's interests. They sign the agreements
and that's what we're going to live. I'm sorry.

00:05:27.319 --> 00:05:34.199
No, the question is, are the women bound by
being muzzled by you? And you could release

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:40.070
them from that immediately, but there's understand
this is not just a question of the mayor's

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:41.080
character.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:46.370
So just taking shot after shot. And what we
learned yesterday was that Michael Bloomberg

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:51.710
did not make his billions winning any debates.
That's for very sure, a very good analysis

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:59.280
of what happened by brand new CNN, political
commentator, Andrew Yang about Bloomberg's

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.650
failure. Uh, here is a Andrew Yang's take
on it.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.669
Yeah. And you know the field the best. You
know what it's like on that stage. What was

00:06:05.669 --> 00:06:10.710
the read? I think three things happened to
Mike tonight. Number one, he just found out

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:15.240
he'd make the debate yesterday. There were
two times when I wasn't sure if I was going

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.139
to make the debate and my team got me together
to prepare. And you're really not sure if

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:22.880
you're preparing cause you're like, I'm not
sure if I'm going to be in this debate. So

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.599
Mike, even though he was, I'm sure getting
coached and prepared, he was like, I don't

00:06:26.599 --> 00:06:30.599
know if I'm going to be in this debate. And
so I don't think he was coached hard enough.

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:34.779
Number two, he was clearly instructed to keep
his cool no matter what. But that ended up

00:06:34.779 --> 00:06:41.129
presenting as being lethargic and on interested
for a big chunk of the debate. And the third

00:06:41.129 --> 00:06:46.099
most telling thing is that if I'm his team,
you know he's going to get a stop in the first

00:06:46.099 --> 00:06:52.199
question like, uh, uh, gender discrimination
or mistreatment question. So you coach him

00:06:52.199 --> 00:06:58.110
and you have him give you 60, 75 second answers
over and over again until he can do it in

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:03.009
his sleep. And the fact that he did not have
those answers at his fingertips, let's me

00:07:03.009 --> 00:07:06.389
know categorically he was not properly prepared
for this debate.

00:07:06.389 --> 00:07:11.819
Now I could go on with the Bloomberg stuff,
but it's all this bad. Before we move on,

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:18.449
if you want to say what good moments did Bloomberg
have? A, I think his best moments were at

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:24.330
one point there, there was a debate about
whether it should be mandatory that employees

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:31.439
be given 20% ownership of the companies where
they work. And Bloomberg said, guys, this

00:07:31.439 --> 00:07:35.319
isn't going to happen and it helps Trump that
we're even talking about this. And I tend

00:07:35.319 --> 00:07:39.330
to agree with him there. We want to democratize
the workplace. There are countless ways to

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:45.650
do at work or representation on boards tying,
you know, the, the multiple of the CEO wage.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:50.080
There's all these different things we can
do. A law that mandates 20% ownership is turned

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:55.940
over to employees. It's just not happening
anytime soon. And making it a subject of debate,

00:07:55.940 --> 00:08:01.930
uh, pushes people away, uh, from any Democrat
I believe. And here is Michael Bloomberg making

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:02.930
that point.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:08.280
Oh, so they can have some say of what happens
to their lives. A large company. Would you

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.749
support what Senator Sanders is proposing?
Absolutely not high. Can't think of a ways

00:08:12.749 --> 00:08:17.310
that would make it easier for Donald Trump
to get reelected and listening to this conversation.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:24.559
It's ridiculous. We're not going to throw
out capitalism. We tried that. Other countries

00:08:24.559 --> 00:08:30.219
tried that. It was called communism and it
just didn't work. So let me make a proposal

00:08:30.219 --> 00:08:34.760
that will work, that has not only support
from then,

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:40.710
uh, at another point, uh, Michael Bloomberg
also, uh, talking about, uh, as you mentioned

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:44.560
there, the, the communism thing, that's a
total red herring and it doesn't make any

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.670
sense to mention that we're not talking about
communism here. Bloomberg is right that a

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:54.100
debate about forcing companies to give 20%
of their ownership away is a, right now the

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:59.250
communism comment is just dumb. One other
moment that was sort of okay for Bloomberg,

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:04.709
although it ended up falling flat, was bringing
up this tired old thing about Bernie's three

00:09:04.709 --> 00:09:05.709
houses.

00:09:05.709 --> 00:09:09.649
It's a wonderful country. We have the best
known socialists in the country, happens to

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:14.660
be a millionaire with three houses. What I
miss here, well, you'll miss that. I work

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:19.550
in Washington. Pouts watch the first prop
live in Burlington house toad. That's good.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:23.740
And like thousands of other Vermonters. I
do have a sub, a cap to give to me for that.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:28.910
Whatever is your home, which talks where it's
tax Haven, New York, New York city. Thank

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.760
you very much. I pay for my tax.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.790
So I don't know. I mean maybe by some standard
it was okay for Bloomberg, but it was really,

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:42.829
really bad overall. The one thing that Bernie,
that Bloomberg's presence did do was energized

00:09:42.829 --> 00:09:48.089
just about everybody on the stage. Even Joe
Biden was fired up in the first part of the

00:09:48.089 --> 00:09:53.740
debate. Warren had higher energy. If Biden
had had yesterday's level of energy throughout

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:59.130
the debates, he might not have tanked his
polling the way that it has. The other really

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:06.800
weird dynamic was mayor Pete versus Amy Klobuchar
getting into some very odd exchanges, including

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:13.070
Pete hammering her about forgetting the president
of Mexico's name. Really going hard on that.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:19.410
And I said that I made an error. Um, I think
having a president that maybe is humble and

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:24.440
is able to admit that here and there maybe
wouldn't be a bad thing, but if you could

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:26.680
let me a response

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:32.980
trivial. I actually didn't know how many members
were in the Knesset, but you're staking your

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:39.110
candidacy on your Washington experience. You're
on the committee that oversees border security.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:43.730
You're on the committee that does trade. You're
literally in part of the committee that's

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:48.180
overseeing these things and we're not able
to speak to literally the first thing about

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:52.410
the politics of the country to ourselves.
Are you trying to say that I'm dumb or are

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.190
you mocking me here, Pete? I'm saying

00:10:54.190 --> 00:11:03.170
I made an error. People sometimes forget names.
I am the one that has, number one, has the

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:09.250
experience based on passing over. One thing.
If I could respond, this was a pretty big

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:14.329
allegation. He's basically saying that I don't
have the experience to be president of the

00:11:14.329 --> 00:11:19.881
United States. I have passed over 100 bills
as the lead Democrat. Since being in the U

00:11:19.881 --> 00:11:26.260
S Senate, I am the one not you, that has won
statewide in congressional district after

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:33.070
congressional district. And I will say when
you tried in Indiana, Pete, to run what happened

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:39.680
to you, you lost by over 20 points to someone
who later lost in my friend Joe Donnelly.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.949
So don't tell me about experience. What unites
us here is we want to win.

00:11:43.949 --> 00:11:48.970
I genuinely don't know who that was good or
bad for because it was also weird and Pete

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:51.220
just continuing to go after her on this issue.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.223
All right, and I think we should put a proven
winner and charge of that ticket. This is

00:11:55.223 --> 00:12:01.910
a race for print. This is the race for president.
If winning a race for Senate in Minnesota

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:05.980
translated directly to becoming president,
I would have grown up under the presidency

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:10.579
of Walter Mondale. This is different and the
reason that I think we need to talk about

00:12:10.579 --> 00:12:16.570
Washington experience is that we should ask
what that experience has led to experiences.

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:20.279
And certainly tenure is not always the same
thing as judgment. We're going to talk about

00:12:20.279 --> 00:12:27.329
votes in the Senate in Washington. Let's talk
about [inaudible]

00:12:27.329 --> 00:12:31.339
a lot of talking over each other there and
that was another unfortunate theme of the

00:12:31.339 --> 00:12:36.850
night candidates and moderators constantly
talking over each other. Many of the candidates

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:41.730
being cut short in their answers. One of the
moderators, Chuck Todd Todd almost constantly

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:48.250
with a running soundtrack of, yeah. Yeah,
and okay. Okay. Over the candidates answers

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:53.630
extremely, extremely annoying. Bernie had
a lot of good moments. For example, this one,

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:58.060
defending workers again in the face of Bloomberg,
the target for the night

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:05.339
to make policy. It is my policy and I'm very
proud of that policy. What we need to do to

00:13:05.339 --> 00:13:11.120
deal with this grotesque level of income and
wealth inequality is to make sure that those

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:15.850
people who are working for Bloomberg wasn't
you who made all that money. Maybe your work

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:25.370
is playing some role in that as well. Those
workers are able to share the benefits. Awesome.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:31.670
When we have so many people go to work every
day and they feel not good about their jobs,

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:37.279
they feel like cogs in a machine. I want workers
to be able to sit on corporate boards as well

00:13:37.279 --> 00:13:41.210
so they can have some safe of what happens
to their lives.

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:45.949
Another strong Bernie moment calling out mayor
Pete in sort of clearer, more direct terms

00:13:45.949 --> 00:13:52.500
this time about his billionaire or billionaire
donors. Pete visibly sweating during this

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.589
moment. When you see the closeup of Pete,
he's sweating badly

00:13:54.589 --> 00:14:03.810
if speaking to the needs and the pain of a
long neglected working class is polarizing.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:10.819
I think you got the wrong work. Well we are
trying finally to do is to give a voice to

00:14:10.819 --> 00:14:18.550
people who after 45 years of work are not
making a nickel more than they did 45 years

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:23.060
ago. We are giving a voice for people who
will say we are sick and tired of billionaires

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:29.070
like mr Bloomberg saying huge expansions of
their wealth while a half a million people

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:34.870
sleep out on the street tonight and that's
all we are saying. Jake is maybe a time for

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:39.810
the working class for this country to have
a little bit of power in Washington rather

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.509
than your billionaire campaign contributors.

00:14:43.509 --> 00:14:49.759
Very, very strong Bernie throughout. I would
probably say Bernie and Warren were the, uh,

00:14:49.759 --> 00:14:56.870
last night, um, with, uh, Joe Biden close
behind just showing more energy, uh, than

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:02.899
he has in recent times. Pete and Amy sort
of going after each other on weird things.

00:15:02.899 --> 00:15:09.319
And then Bloomberg really the loser of the
night. So if you say to me, uh, who is the,

00:15:09.319 --> 00:15:14.769
uh, in the net, who's the biggest winner last
night? I actually believe it's Donald Trump.

00:15:14.769 --> 00:15:20.260
Donald Trump net benefited the most from last
night's debate because Bloomberg completely

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:25.129
self-destructed and we'll see what this does
to his campaign. The other candidate has got

00:15:25.129 --> 00:15:30.070
shots in it, Bloomberg, but otherwise, I don't
believe the needle will be greatly moved for

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:35.199
anybody else because we're so far into this
thing. And the only new presence on the stage

00:15:35.199 --> 00:15:42.189
was Michael Bloomberg and overall the what
will be portrayed as infighting by the right,

00:15:42.189 --> 00:15:46.930
even though it was making very clear the different
types of choices that are available.

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:52.470
I think in the net, Trump comes away with
a slight benefit from last night's debates.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:56.259
Just my opinion and you can disagree with
me and we can all be reasonable, respectful

00:15:56.259 --> 00:16:01.730
people on that issue. Did a poll on my Twitter
about 4,000 of you voted. The question was

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:07.950
how does tonight's debate affect Bloomberg's
polling? A plurality, nearly 40% said Bloomberg

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:12.110
loses eight or more points as a result of
last night's performance. I don't think it's

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:17.550
actually that bad, but I do think Bloomberg
loses a bit, maybe has to spend another hundred

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:23.750
million to gain it back. I did not expect
Bloomberg to get as annihilated as he did

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:28.800
last night and one other thought that I had
about last night's debate. Last night's debate

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:33.750
was by far the most entertaining debate of
the primary, but we also probably heard the

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:40.550
least about policy from the candidates. Detailed
policy. It was a lot of attacks on Bloomberg,

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:45.520
Pete and Amy arguing about experience and
how bad it is not to know the name of the

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:46.769
president of Mexico.

00:16:46.769 --> 00:16:52.480
You had a lot of talking point attacks. That's
probably in some way emblematic of our politics

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:56.790
and media today. Sadly, the most entertaining
to watch debate whose ratings may have been

00:16:56.790 --> 00:17:02.329
very, very good, had the least substantive
policy discussion of any recent debate. I

00:17:02.329 --> 00:17:09.180
can remember certainly ideological differences
and draw lines in the sand were drawn, but

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:12.850
maybe in some way this is part of the problem
that we're having in the discourse in the

00:17:12.850 --> 00:17:18.460
United States. I guess the major thing we
learned of new information is Michael Bloomberg

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:23.069
is not a good debater. Let me know your thoughts.
Let me know what you expect to be the implications

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:28.270
and repercussions to last night's debate.
I'm on Twitter at the Pacman. They'll take

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:29.270
a quick break

